(docs/BUGS_FOUND.md #90. A buffer parameter handed on to a second function is
 still the ORIGINAL caller's buffer, so a reallocation two — or thirty —
 calls deep has to reach the variable that owns it. A call passes on the cell
 its own parameter arrived with, so the innermost frame writes straight to the
 owner at the moment of the reallocation, and every frame in between refreshes
 its own copy of the pointer on the way back out.)

To 'pad out' with a buffer called sink.
    append "0123456789" to sink.

To 'pad out twice' with a buffer called sink.
    'pad out' of sink.
    'pad out' of sink.

To 'pad out down to' with a buffer called sink and a number called remaining.
    If remaining is greater than 0,
        'pad out twice' of sink,
        a number called left is remaining subtract 1,
        'pad out down to' of sink and left.

a buffer called doubled is "start".
a number called rounds is 0.
While rounds is less than 300,
    'pad out twice' of doubled,
    Set rounds to rounds add 1.
Print "two calls deep: {doubled's size}".

a buffer called deepened is "start".
'pad out down to' of deepened and 300.
Print "three hundred deep: {deepened's size}".
